6,0$a) Distinctive pleasant odour, as of pears$gr. pyriodora
6,7$b) Odour différent (not pleasant odour, as of pears)
7,0$a) Tendency to redden when handled$gr. godeyi
7,8$b) Surface unchanging when handled
8,9$a) Lilaceous-violet or very light colours, even whitish, but then with an almost smooth cap cuticle also at maturity; stem devoid of pinkish-red tinges
8,10$b) Species with différent characters (not :Lilaceous-violet or very light colours, even whitish, but then with an almost smooth cap cuticle also at maturity; stem devoid of pinkish-red tinges)
9,0$a) Cap almost white or else darker, even lilacine, cuticle always very smooth, exceptional in the genus înocybe$gr. geophylla
9,0$b) Stem, flesh, gills and cap (the last less intense) variably lilac-violet; cap usually squamose or fibrillose$gr. obscura
10,11$a) Stem characteristic, either in its progressive darkening or spiny ornamentation
10,12$b) Stem devoid of the above-mentioned characters
11,0$a) Stem displaying smoke-brown patches or darkening ail over with âge, starting from its base$gr. atripes
11,0$b) Stem entirely covered (and so is the cap) with erect, spike-like squames$I. hystrix
12,13$a) Stem covered with whitish scurfiness, in the upper part at least, rich in bunches of cystidia
12,22$b) Stem not pruinose in any région
13,14$a) Gills exhibiting a yellow tint, especially when young (concolorous, as a rule, with other régions of carpophore) $gr. lutescens
 13,14$b) Gills without yellow hues
14,15$a) Stem normally exhibiting a variably patent reddish-pink tinge in its upper portion, appearing beneath pruinose whitish surface, at least in some life stages; this colour being usually présent in the corresponding flesh even when absent on the outside
14,19$b) Stem devoid of any reddish-pink, both outside and inside, in any life period
15,16$a) Cap generally quite light, (somewhat isabel-ivory-whitisb ) or else more or less deep ochre to brown, but then with cuticle always definitely squamulose with more or less erect scales
15,18$b) Cap, as a rule, darker, almost chestnut-brown, at least partially; cuticle never broken up into erect squames, but with fibrillosity in rods or bundles almost always appressed (only exceptionally are single fibres revolute at tips)
16,17$a) Average microscopical détails; cystidia may be stumpy only .
16,0$b) Microscopical détails almost always deviating from average pattern: either cystidia oblong, rod-shaped, neckless, often with intermediate constrictions, or spores much elongate and thin .$gr. furfurea
17,0$a) Smell pleasant, of bitter almond or similar to it$gr. hirtella 
17,0$b) Smell différent, most often stale, spermatîc$gr. eutheles
18,0$a) Cap strong tawny-cbestnut-brown (exceptionally yellowish); cuticle with considerably regular, radially arranged fibrils splitting only with âge$gr. friesii
18,20$b) Cap colours lighter, brown and chestnut not prevailing being mixed with ochre or beige; smooth or a bit rough cuticle, due either to fibril arrangement in bundles or slightly revolute at tips . . gr. virgatula
 19,0$a) Flesh brownish inside the stem; cap cuticle fairly smooth $gr. vîscidula 
19,20$b) Flesh inside the stem whitish; cap cuticle smooth to rugged on account of more or less erect squamules
20,0$a) Cap brownish mixed variably with other hues; stem hollow-fïstulous at maturity$gr. descissa
20,21$b) Species with other characters
21,0$a) Cap colours more or less bright, deep yellow always prédominant; stem solid to hollow at maturity$gr. auricoma
21,0$b) Cap colours more varied, yellow not prédominant, when present; stem consistently solid even at maturity, often of light or very light tints, like the inner flesh$gr. terrifera
22,0$a) Cap cuticle normally rough, exhibiting more or less raised fibres either single or matted into squamules; spores long (18 jxm maximum), more or less narrow, often ovoid-cylindrical$gr. lacera
22,0$b) Cap cuticle smoother than usual, as a rule, with a single exception; spores normal in size, never over 11-12.5 µm in length $gr. phaeodisca 
23,0$a) Surface and flesh turning variably pinkish-red when eut or handled; smell pleasant, reminiscent of pears$gr. bresadolae
23,24$b) Surface and flesh without any evident colour altération; différent smell (not : smell pleasant, reminiscent of pears)
24,25 $a) Spores tboroughly peculiar, globose or subglobose, thrown out into rows of long aculei radially arranged$I. calospora
24,25$b) Spores not globose, nodules never sharp and prominent as those of the preceding species
25,0$a) Growing on wood (logs or timber)$gr. lanuginosa
25,26$b) Terricolous species
26,27$a) Stem base with an evident and more or less emarginated bulb 
26,29$b) Stem base without a differentiated bulb, at most progressively enlarging or not at ail 
27,0$a) Cap light to more or less yellow, never decidedly brown; stem lighter than the cap; stem base frequently exhibiting marginate bulb .$gr. praetervisa
27,28$b) Cap lacking yellow tinges: chestnut-brown exceptionally tending to tobacco- beige; stem ochre (normally with a more or less evident pinkish shade to brown even rather strong); stem base not always emarginated 
28,0$a) Stem inside or out (or both) pinkish (exceptionally chestnut) with normally emarginated bulb$gr. grammata
28,30$b) Stem devoid of any pink tinge either in- or outside, often with prominent bulb, but never definitely (except one species) emarginated $gr. umbrina 
29,30$a) Cap cuticle very smooth, consisting of closely appressed fibrils slightly separating with âge radially
29,33$b) Cap cuticle with coarser fibres, assembled into rimose (exceptionally squamose) bundles variably raised
30,31$a) Cap lacking evident remnants of cortina, even when young
30,32$b) Cap with evident cortina remnants appearing like a tomentose veil, at least when young
31,0$a) Cap always very light, white to cream-ivory$gr. umbratica
31,0$b) Cap stramineous-white (in carpophores of exceptional large size in the in the genus Inocybe) to brownish-chestnut$gr. fibrosa
32,0$a) Carpophore minute. Cap as rule not exceeding 2 cm in diameter; growing in forests$gr. petiginosa
32,0$b) Carpophore larger; cap up to 4-5 cm in diameter; growing on sandy soil (either marine or of marine origin)$gr. decipiens
33,0$a) Cap tawny-orange-yellow; cuticle with rimose fibres, but never raised into squamules$gr. viscosissima
33,34$b) Cap brownish or chestnut mixed with ochraceous yellow; cuticle occasionally a bit squamulose
34,0$a) Cystidia globose (ail or most of them), virtually or definitely neckless$gr. decipientoides
34,0$b) Cystidia most frequently bottle-shaped, always with a well differentiated neck$gr. Pseudoasterospora

